</think> \begin{align*}
3\Delta + 3\bigcirc &= 21\\
\implies \Delta + \bigcirc &= 7 \quad \text{(divide both sides by 3)}\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \textbf{Equation 1}\\
3\bigcirc + 4\Delta &= 16\\
\implies \bigcirc + \frac{4}{3}\Delta &= \frac{16}{3} \quad \text{(divide both sides by 3)}\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \textbf{Equation 2}
\end{align*} Now, we will subtract Equation 1 from Equation 2 to eliminate $\bigcirc$ and find the value of $\Delta$. We get \begin{align*}
&\left(\bigcirc + \frac{4}{3}\Delta\right) - \left(\Delta + \bigcirc\right) = \frac{16}{3} - 7\\
\implies &\frac{1}{3}\Delta = -\frac{5}{3}\\
\implies &\Delta = -5
\end{align*} Now that we know the value of $\Delta$, we can substitute it back into Equation 1 to find the value of $\bigcirc$. We get \begin{align*}
\Delta + \bigcirc &= 7\\
-5 + \bigcirc &= 7\\
\bigcirc &= 12
\end{align*} Now that we have found the values of $\Delta$ and $\bigcirc$, we can find the sum of two circles. The sum of two circles is $2\bigcirc = 2\cdot12 = \boxed{24}$. </answer>
